Z||000000||Adrift||12-12-2001||12:01 AM||||I picked up my rebuilt calipers and new rotors today - here's my question though. There are many parts of my old caliper which don't appear on my new caliper (See below) - is it normal to reuse these parts, or were they supposed to come with the rebuilt caliper as well?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP_1648.JPG">  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Joe Torma||12-12-2001||12:17 AM||||My rebuilt calipers didn't come with the e-brake pieces either.  Just reuse them.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||extinct||12-12-2001||12:52 AM||||But what about the attaching bolts/sleeves and seal.  Is he suppose to reuse them too?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||12-12-2001||12:58 AM||||Huh...just noticed that.  What exactly is missing?  Just the bolt boot?  That pops off.  You can get new slide bolts if you want, but they should be fine.  Lightly lube them.  Are there o-rings in the new caliper <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||extinct||12-12-2001||01:17 AM||||If he would have bought a "loaded rebuilt caliper" would that have come with the attaching bolts/sleeves and boot seal?<P>I want to replace the rear calipers on my 88 formula.  If the "loaded" once come with all new hardaware (bolts/sleaves/boots) then it would be worth the extra money to get the loaded ones rather than the unloaded.  Plus  you don't have to buy pads and springs/sliders.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||12-12-2001||01:31 AM||||More often than not, loaded calipers are the best deal.  Not only do you save time (just lube them up in the proper areas and bolt them on), but you're also guaranteed that the parts fit right and you don't have to waste your time with any stupid tiny parts breaking or flingin off into who knows where.<P>Some loaded calipers have bolts, some don't.  It depnds on the design and the manufacturer.  They're usually very reusable; get some anti-sieze (silver compound you can buy anywhere) or sil-glyde (silicone based lube availabe in limited locations) to lube up the bolt bushings.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||NEPTUNE||12-12-2001||08:59 AM||||I bought a rear hardware kit from a supplier recommended by my Pontiac dealer parts dept. <BR>$25 (USD) includes the big round "spring" that holds the inner pad to the piston, rubber bushings,bleeder caps, all those little peicesfor both sides. I would recommend replacing these little parts- they don't last forever!  You do need to re use the e brake levers, though.<P>[This message has been edited by NEPTUNE (edited 12-12-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by NEPTUNE (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
